# Stellux Style

Vendored code is exempt and must never be restyled: doom, python, vim,
dropbear, bearssl, stb_* headers, and kernel/boot/limine.h.

## Language, in code and comments

- ASCII only. No unicode arrows, icons, emoji, box drawing, or typographic
  punctuation anywhere.
- No em-dashes. Use commas, parentheses, or separate sentences.
- No semicolons in comments. Restructure the sentence with commas or split it.
- Plain, simple language, written for a reader with no prior context in this
  domain. Banned words and their relatives: leverage, robust, seamless,
  elegant, comprehensive, powerful, simply, cutting-edge.
- Comments are timeless. Never reference the current change, commit, PR,
  refactor, conversation, or anything "new", "old", or "now". A comment must
  still be true and useful in five years.

## Comments

- Explain intent and the why behind the decision, never mechanics the code
  already shows. If the code is self-explanatory, write no comment.
- One to two lines maximum, counting content lines only (the /** and */
  frame lines of a block comment do not count). Two exceptions: doxygen
  blocks on public APIs (@brief, @param, @return), and rare longer blocks
  documenting a subtle invariant, race, or hardware contract that genuinely
  needs the space, like the critical section note in dynpriv lower(). Length
  never excuses narration, a long comment must still explain why.
- Hand-written hardware spec quotations and RFC or protocol reference blocks
  (the xhci headers, net/ RFC notes) are protected documentation. Never edit
  them in any way, not even to shorten, reword, or normalize punctuation.
- Privileged functions add `@note Privilege: **required**` to their doxygen
  block (owned by the dynpriv rule).
- Comments never interrupt a single statement. An initializer list, argument
  list, or expression is one atom, so a comment sits above the whole statement
  or trails a definition line. Per-field trailing comments are welcome in
  struct definitions and constant tables, and forbidden inside initializations.
- Single space before inline comments: `x = 5; // Reason`. Exception:
  column-aligned comment blocks in constant and register tables align within
  their block.
- No decorative separators such as `// =====` or `// -----`.

## Stanza formatting

Function bodies read as stanzas: groups of one to six statements that
accomplish one sub-step (validate, acquire, initialize, mutate, commit,
return), separated by exactly one blank line.

- Blank line after an early-exit guard block. Consecutive guards are
  separate stanzas, one blank line between each.
- Blank line between acquiring a resource and using it.
- Blank line before the final return, unless the body is a single stanza.
- No blank line inside a stanza, and never two consecutive blank lines.
- Bodies of one to three lines form a single stanza with no blank lines.

```cpp
// BAD, one undifferentiated block
void* mem = heap::kzalloc(sizeof(T));
if (!mem) {
    return strong_ref<T>();
}
T* obj = new (mem) T(static_cast<args_t&&>(args)...);
return strong_ref<T>::adopt(obj);

// GOOD, allocation guarded, then construction
void* mem = heap::kzalloc(sizeof(T));
if (!mem) {
    return strong_ref<T>();
}

T* obj = new (mem) T(static_cast<args_t&&>(args)...);
return strong_ref<T>::adopt(obj);
```

## Naming

- snake_case for functions, variables, types, namespaces, and files.
  SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for constants and macros.
- Private and protected class members carry the m_ prefix. Trailing
  underscores are forbidden.
- namespace = directory = module name (see the arch-layout rule).
- Resource providers are named for what they wrap, X_provider wraps X:
  file_provider wraps fs::file, shmem_provider wraps mm::shmem.
- using aliases end in _t only when aliasing a scalar such as an address or
  id: phys_addr_t. Composite types take plain names.

## Structure

- Struct definitions and file-scope globals sit at the top of the file, above
  all function definitions, in headers and in .cpp files alike.
- struct for plain data and intrusive nodes, class only when private state
  guards an invariant.
- Anonymous namespaces exist only to give internal linkage to types. Functions
  and variables use file-scope static instead. Test files always use
  file-scope static. At most one anonymous namespace per file: hoist
  type-independent constants above it and place type-dependent constants
  (sizeof-derived and similar) directly below it.
- Class methods containing logic are defined in the class's own .cpp file.
  Only one-line accessors and setters are defined inline in headers. Templates
  are header-only by necessity and exempt.
- Include guards in the kernel: STELLUX_<DIR>_<FILE>_H, matching the file
  path. Userland projects use their project prefix, as in STLXGFX_WINDOW_H.
- K&R braces, 4-space indent, pointer binds to the type: `task* t`.
- Soft line limit of 120 columns.

## Errors and logging

- Per-module error constants in the module header: OK = 0 and negative
  int32_t ERR_* values.
- Crossing an error vocabulary boundary requires an explicit translation
  function, as map_fs_error_to_resource does for fs to resource codes.
- [[nodiscard]] on functions returning ownership or a lock guard.
- log::fatal only for unrecoverable boot failures. Optional subsystems log a
  warning and degrade.

## Low level

- In C++ code, C++ casts only, never C-style casts. Plain `(void)expr`
  discards are fine. C sources are exempt.
- volatile only for MMIO and memory shared with hardware.
- All magic numbers become named constexpr constants.
